<?xml version="1.0"?>
<root main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">

    <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1000" name="NavigateRecovery">

      <PipelineSequence name="NavigateWithReplanning">
        <!-- Raised from 3.0 - was throttling global replans to every ~333ms
             despite NTFields (planner_server/GridBased) itself running in
             well under 30ms per call (benchmarked directly on this Jetson,
             see argo_sonic_nav.py history). 20.0 matches controller_server's
             own controller_frequency below it in nav2.yaml - there's no
             benefit replanning faster than the local MPPI loop that
             actually consumes {path} can react to it. NTFields has no live
             obstacle awareness of its own (trained only on the static map,
             no costmap subscription) - this makes it recompute a fresh
             route from the robot's ACTUAL current pose much sooner after a
             deviation or a recovery backup/spin, not "notice" a dynamic
             obstacle mid-corridor - that's still entirely MPPI/local-costmap's job. -->
        <RateController hz="20.0">
          <ComputePathToPose goal="{goal}" path="{path}" planner_id="GridBased"/>
        </RateController>
        <FollowPath path="{path}" controller_id="FollowPath"/>
      </PipelineSequence>

      <!-- Recovery. RoundRobin, not Sequence: each failure runs the NEXT
           recovery rather than all of them every time, and it only reports
           failure once every option has been tried. A Sequence would abort
           the whole navigation the first time BackUp couldn't run (someone
           standing behind the robot is enough), which is exactly the case
           recovery exists to survive.

           Clearing the costmap first is the cheap, motionless option: it
           gives the planner fresh obstacle data to route around without
           the robot moving at all. Backing up is the fallback for when
           that isn't enough and the robot needs to physically unwedge.

           backup_dist/backup_speed live HERE, not in nav2.yaml's
           behavior_server block — nav2_behaviors/BackUp reads both from
           the action goal, and it's this BT node that fills it in (see
           back_up_action.hpp's InputPorts). 0.10 m is a deliberate nudge
           clear, not a retreat: the rear is covered only by the two rear
           ultrasonics (safety_shield.py), so a long blind reverse is the
           one motion this robot has the least sensing for. 0.10 m/s over
           10 cm is ~1 s of travel, well inside the BT node's default 10 s
           time_allowance.

           This replaces the mppi_reverse_controller experiment, which was
           removed: it only ever set vx_min from _check_recovery_state(),
           which keyed off last_clear_time, which was only written by
           clear_costmap_hook() — and nothing ever called that. The flag
           stayed 0.0 for the life of the process, so the node never
           enabled reverse once. Reversing during recovery is what BackUp
           already does properly, through behavior_server, with collision
           checking (simulate_ahead_time) that a raw vx_min change has no
           equivalent of. -->
      <RoundRobin name="RecoveryActions">
        <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
        <BackUp name="BackUpTenCm" backup_dist="0.10" backup_speed="0.10"/>
      </RoundRobin>

    </RecoveryNode>

  </BehaviorTree>
</root>